        <body>
    <h1>Student Rights, Discipline, Manifestation Determination, & Due Process in District 20 (NYC) - A Parent's Guide</h1>

    <p>District 20, encompassing Bay Ridge, Bensonhurst, Borough Park, Dyker Heights, and Fort Hamilton, is committed to upholding student rights and ensuring fair disciplinary procedures. Understanding these rights is crucial for both students and parents, particularly when dealing with disciplinary actions, manifestation determinations, and due process hearings. This guide provides essential information and actionable steps for navigating these complex situations within District 20.</p>

    <h2>I. Manifestation Determination Review (MDR): Protecting Students with IEPs and 504 Plans</h2>

    <p>The Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) is a critical safeguard for students with disabilities who face disciplinary action. According to the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State regulations (Part 200.2(b)(1)(viii)), an MDR is required when a student with an Individualized Education Program (IEP) or a 504 plan is facing suspension exceeding 10 <i>consecutive</i> school days or when there is a pattern of removals that constitute a change of placement.  A "pattern of removals" can occur even if no single suspension exceeds 10 days, so long as the total time out of school adds up to more than 10 days within a school year and constitutes a significant change to the student's educational program. These removals can be suspensions or other disciplinary actions that remove the student from their current placement.</p>

    <h3>What is the Purpose of the MDR?</h3>

    <p>The purpose of the MDR is to determine whether the student's misconduct was a direct result of their disability or if the school failed to properly implement the IEP/504 plan. The review team must consider all relevant information, including evaluation and diagnostic results, relevant information supplied by the parents of the child, observations of the child, and the child’s IEP and placement. (34 CFR § 300.530(e)(1)).</p>

    <h3>Your Rights During the MDR Process: A Step-by-Step Guide</h3>

    <ol>
        <li><strong>Notification:</strong> The school must notify you of the decision to conduct an MDR within a reasonable timeframe after the disciplinary action is taken, but definitely no later than the date of the change of placement (i.e. the 11th cumulative day of suspension).</li>
        <li><strong>Active Participation:</strong> Parents have the <i>right</i> to participate in the MDR process. Your input is vital.  Bring all relevant documentation, including private evaluations, medical records, and any communication with the school regarding your child's disability and IEP/504 plan implementation.</li>
        <li><strong>Documentation is Key:</strong> Prepare a written statement outlining your concerns about the school's implementation of the IEP/504 plan and how it may have contributed to the behavior in question.</li>
        <li><strong>Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE):</strong> If you believe the school's evaluations are inadequate, you have the right to request an IEE at public expense (under certain conditions, outlined in Part 200.5(g) of the NYS regulations).  Requesting an IEE *before* the MDR can provide valuable independent data to present at the meeting.</li>
        <li><strong>Understanding the "But For" Standard:</strong> The MDR team must determine if the conduct in question was "caused by or had a direct and substantial relationship to the child's disability" OR "was the direct result of the local educational agency’s (LEA) failure to implement the IEP." If either of these conditions are met, the behavior is considered a manifestation of the disability. In plain language, the question is: “But for” the student’s disability, would the behavior have occurred?</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>What Happens If the Behavior IS a Manifestation of the Disability?</h3>

    <p>If the MDR team determines that the behavior was a manifestation of the disability, the IEP team must conduct a functional behavioral assessment (FBA), unless one has already been conducted. The IEP team must also implement a behavior intervention plan (BIP) for the student, or review the existing BIP and modify it, as necessary, to address the behavior.  Furthermore, the student must be returned to their placement prior to the disciplinary action, <i>unless</i> the parents and the LEA agree to a change of placement as part of the modification of the BIP (34 CFR § 300.530(f)).</p>

    <h3>What Happens If the Behavior is NOT a Manifestation of the Disability?</h3>

    <p>If the MDR team determines that the behavior was NOT a manifestation of the disability, the student can be disciplined in the same manner as students without disabilities. However, even in these cases, the school must continue to provide FAPE (Free Appropriate Public Education) to the student.  This means that during any period of suspension or expulsion, the student is entitled to continue to receive services to enable them to continue to participate in the general education curriculum, although in another setting, and to progress toward meeting the goals set out in the child’s IEP (34 CFR § 300.530(d)).</p>

    <h3>Common District 20 MDR Pitfalls & How to Avoid Them:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Predetermined Outcomes:</strong> Be wary of MDR meetings where the outcome seems predetermined.  Advocate assertively for your child's rights and ensure that the team genuinely considers all information.</li>
        <li><strong>Lack of Parent Input:</strong> Schools sometimes fail to adequately consider parent input.  Submit your written statement and documentation in advance, and don't be afraid to speak up during the meeting. Record the meeting, if permitted by district policy.</li>
        <li><strong>Inadequate IEP Implementation:</strong> If the IEP is not being followed, this is a significant factor in the MDR.  Document all instances of non-compliance and present them at the meeting.</li>
        <li><strong>Failure to Conduct/Revise FBA/BIP:</strong> Insist on a Functional Behavior Assessment (FBA) and Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) if one doesn't exist or needs revision. Ensure the BIP is data-driven and consistently implemented.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>II. Navigating Suspensions in District 20: Your Rights and Responsibilities</h2>

    <p>Understanding suspension procedures is vital.  New York State Education Law § 3214 outlines the grounds for suspension and the due process rights afforded to students. There are two types of suspensions: short-term (5 days or less) and long-term (more than 5 days).</p>

    <h3>Short-Term Suspensions (5 Days or Less):</h3>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Informal Conference:</strong> The student and the person in parental relation must be given an opportunity for an informal conference with the principal or other person authorized to impose the suspension to discuss the conduct underlying the suspension.</li>
        <li><strong>Written Notification:</strong> The parent must be notified in writing of the reason for the suspension and the period of suspension.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Long-Term Suspensions (More Than 5 Days):</h3>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Written Notice:</strong> The student and parent must be provided with written notice of the charges against the student and the right to a fair hearing.</li>
        <li><strong>Right to a Hearing:</strong> The student has the right to a hearing to challenge the suspension. This hearing must be conducted fairly and impartially.</li>
        <li><strong>Right to Representation:</strong> The student has the right to be represented by counsel at the hearing.</li>
        <li><strong>Right to Present Evidence:</strong> The student has the right to present evidence and witnesses on their behalf.</li>
        <li><strong>Right to Cross-Examine Witnesses:</strong> The student has the right to cross-examine witnesses presented by the school.</li>
        <li><strong>Impartial Hearing Officer:</strong> The hearing must be conducted by an impartial hearing officer.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Step-by-Step Guide to Challenging a Suspension in District 20:</h3>

    <ol>
        <li><strong>Review the Suspension Notice:</strong> Carefully review the suspension notice to understand the charges against your child.</li>
        <li><strong>Request a Hearing:</strong> If you disagree with the suspension, request a hearing in writing within the timeframe specified in the notice. Failing to do so can be deemed a waiver of the right to a hearing.</li>
        <li><strong>Gather Evidence:</strong> Collect any evidence that supports your child's case, such as witness statements, school records, and medical documentation.</li>
        <li><strong>Prepare Your Case:</strong> Prepare a clear and concise statement outlining why the suspension is unwarranted or unfair.</li>
        <li><strong>Attend the Hearing:</strong> Attend the hearing with your child and present your case. Be respectful and professional.</li>
        <li><strong>Appeal the Decision:</strong> If you are not satisfied with the hearing officer's decision, you have the right to appeal to the New York State Commissioner of Education within 30 days of the decision.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>III. Due Process Hearings: Resolving Disputes with District 20</h2>

    <p>A due process hearing is a formal legal proceeding used to resolve disputes between parents and school districts regarding the identification, evaluation, or educational placement of a child with a disability, or the provision of FAPE (Free Appropriate Public Education) to the child (IDEA, 34 CFR § 300.507). This is a last resort option when other methods of dispute resolution, such as IEP meetings and mediation, have failed.</p>

    <h3>When Should You Consider a Due Process Hearing?</h3>

    <p>Consider a due process hearing when:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>The district refuses to conduct an evaluation that you believe is necessary.</li>
        <li>You disagree with the results of the district's evaluation.</li>
        <li>You disagree with the IEP developed by the IEP team.</li>
        <li>The district fails to implement the IEP properly.</li>
        <li>You believe your child is not receiving FAPE.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The Due Process Hearing Process:</h3>

    <ol>
        <li><strong>Filing a Complaint:</strong> The process begins with filing a written due process complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED). The complaint must contain specific information, including the child's name, address, school, a description of the problem, and a proposed resolution. (34 CFR § 300.508)</li>
        <li><strong>Sufficiency Determination:</strong> NYSED reviews the complaint to determine if it meets the requirements of IDEA.</li>
        <li><strong>Resolution Period:</strong> Within 15 days of receiving the complaint, the school district must convene a resolution meeting with the parents and relevant members of the IEP team to attempt to resolve the dispute. You can waive this meeting.</li>
        <li><strong>Hearing Rights:</strong> You have the right to bring an attorney or advocate with you to the resolution meeting and the due process hearing.</li>
        <li><strong>Due Process Hearing:</strong> If the dispute is not resolved during the resolution period, a due process hearing will be scheduled.</li>
        <li><strong>Hearing Officer:</strong> The hearing is conducted by an impartial hearing officer who is not an employee of the school district.</li>
        <li><strong>Evidence and Testimony:</strong> Both sides have the opportunity to present evidence, call witnesses, and cross-examine opposing witnesses.</li>
        <li><strong>Hearing Decision:</strong> The hearing officer will issue a written decision within 45 days of the end of the resolution period (or adjusted timeline if agreed upon by both parties).</li>
        <li><strong>Appeals:</strong> Either party can appeal the hearing officer's decision to a state court or federal district court.</li>
    </ol>

     <h3>Important Considerations Before Filing for Due Process:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Exhaust All Other Avenues:</b> Due process is adversarial. Consider IEP meetings, mediation, and facilitated IEP meetings first. Document all attempts to resolve the issue amicably.</li>
        <li><b>Legal Representation:</b> Seek legal counsel from a special education attorney. The process is complex and requires a thorough understanding of special education law.</li>
        <li><b>Prepare Thoroughly:</b> Gather all relevant documentation, including IEPs, evaluations, school records, and communication with the school district.  Organize your evidence chronologically and by topic.</li>
        <li><b>Understand the Burden of Proof:</b> In most cases, the parent bears the burden of proof.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>IV. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)</h2>

    <dl>
        <dt><strong>Q: What is the difference between an IEP and a 504 plan?</strong></dt>
        <dd>A: An IEP is for students with disabilities who require specialized instruction and related services. A 504 plan is for students with disabilities who need accommodations to access the general education curriculum. IEPs are covered under IDEA, while 504 Plans are covered under Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973.</dd>
        <dt><strong>Q: Can my child be suspended for behaviors related to their disability?</strong></dt>
        <dd>A: Not if the behavior is determined to be a manifestation of their disability. The MDR process determines this.</dd>
        <dt><strong>Q: What should I do if I disagree with the school's evaluation of my child?</strong></dt>
        <dd>A: You have the right to request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense under certain conditions. Contact the school district's special education department for more information.</dd>
        <dt><strong>Q: How can I get a copy of my child's school records?</strong></dt>
        <dd>A: You have the right to access your child's school records under the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA). Submit a written request to the school principal.</dd>
        <dt><strong>Q: What resources are available to help me advocate for my child in District 20?</strong></dt>
        <dd>A: You can contact local parent advocacy organizations, legal aid organizations, and the New York State Education Department's Office of Special Education.</dd>
    </dl>

    <p>By understanding your rights and responsibilities, you can effectively advocate for your child and ensure they receive a fair and appropriate education in District 20.</p>
</body>
